<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polisario Front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Frente Polisario</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polisario Front is a Sahrawi nationalist movement founded in May 1973 to end Spanish rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> handed the territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, it proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahrawi-arab-democratic-republic">Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic</a> in February 1976 and fought both countries, forcing Mauritania to withdraw in 1979. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> ceasefire with Morocco in 1991 promised a referendum that never took place, and fighting resumed in 2020.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polisario-front.jpg" alt="Polisario Front" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: El Uali Mustafá Sayed from Polisario Front · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Sahrawi_Arab_Democratic_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1971 a group of young Sahrawi students in the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> began organising what became the Embryonic Movement for the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saguia-el-hamra">Saguia el-Hamra</a> and Río de Oro. It sought backing from Arab governments, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and Morocco, but drew only faint notice of support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, and so moved to Spanish Sahara to begin an armed rebellion.</p><p>The Polisario Front was formally constituted on 10 May 1973 at Ain Bentili by several Sahrawi university students, survivors of the 1968 massacres in Zouérat and Sahrawi veterans of the Spanish Army. On 20 May it attacked El-Khanga, where a post of the Tropas Nómadas, Sahrawi-staffed auxiliary forces, was overrun and its rifles seized. Its power grew from early 1975 as the Tropas Nómadas began deserting to it. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> visiting mission in June 1975 found an overwhelming consensus among Sahrawis for independence and judged the Polisario the most powerful political force in the territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>'s Green March of 6 November 1975, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> signed the Madrid Accords, ceding Spanish Sahara to Morocco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. The Polisario proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahrawi-arab-democratic-republic">Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic</a> on 27 February 1976 and fought both armies. Moroccan air raids on refugee camps led it to move the refugees to Tindouf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. With arms and funds from Algeria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> its army grew to several thousand fighters using hit-and-run attacks.</p><p>A peace treaty of 5 August 1979 had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> recognise Sahrawi rights and withdraw its forces; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> annexed the area Mauritania left. From the mid-1980s Morocco built a long sand wall, the Moroccan Wall, enclosing the economically useful parts of the territory, which stalemated the war. A ceasefire monitored by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> mission MINURSO took effect on 6 September 1991 on the promise of a referendum, which stalled over who could vote. The ceasefire was ended in November 2020.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Polisario describes itself as a front covering all political trends in Sahrawi society, not a party, and has no party programme. It is a Sahrawi nationalist and Arab socialist movement, and in 1991 it voted for free market economics and multi-party politics. Its secretary general is elected by a General Popular Congress, which meets every four years and draws delegates from the popular congresses of the refugee camps near Tindouf, from women's, youth and workers' organisations and from the army. Between congresses a National Secretariat is the supreme body. There is no formal membership: anyone who works with it or lives in the camps counts as a member. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> considers it the legitimate representative of the Sahrawi people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polisario Front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Polisario Front</b>, formed in 1973 to win independence for the Spanish Sahara, fought Morocco and Mauritania after Spain withdrew in 1975 and Moroccan forces occupied the territory; Mauritania gave up its claim in 1979, and Polisario claimed to have killed more than 5,000 Moroccan soldiers between 1982…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Polisario Front</b>, formed in 1973, fought Spanish rule in Spanish Sahara.</blockquote><a href="/state/sahrawi-arab-democratic-republic">Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Polisario Front</b> launched an independence struggle in 1973, while Morocco and Mauritania claimed the territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-sahara">Spanish Sahara</a></li></ul></section>
